WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.611
- When everyone is doing the same thing

00:00:01.611 --> 00:00:03.476
and everyone's competing in the same realm

00:00:03.476 --> 00:00:05.215
you have to think much bigger.

00:00:05.215 --> 00:00:07.074
There's a lot less competition up there.

00:00:07.074 --> 00:00:08.500
There's nobody up there, actually.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.722
Don't wait to be inspired
to do the action.

00:00:10.722 --> 00:00:12.961
You have to do the action first
and then you'll be inspired.

00:00:12.961 --> 00:00:15.439
And I say in the ashes
of all your failures,

00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:18.640
if you look close enough
you'll find keys to success.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.986
- He's an American film maker,

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.425
screenwriter and musician.

00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:23.932
He directed the action film El Mariachi

00:00:23.932 --> 00:00:26.001
which grossed two million dollars against

00:00:26.001 --> 00:00:28.371
a budget of $7,000.

00:00:28.371 --> 00:00:30.044
He's a friend and frequent collaborator

00:00:30.044 --> 00:00:31.621
with Quentin Tarantino.

00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:33.693
He's Robert Rodriguez and here's my take

00:00:33.693 --> 00:00:35.613
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:00:35.613 --> 00:00:37.758
Rule number 10 is my personal favorite

00:00:37.758 --> 00:00:39.299
and I'm curious to figure out which one

00:00:39.299 --> 00:00:40.765
you guys like the best.

00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:42.819
And always guys, as
you're watching the videos

00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:45.003
if you hear something the
really resonates with you

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:46.826
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:46.826 --> 00:00:48.325
and put quotes around
it so that other people

00:00:48.325 --> 00:00:49.795
can be inspired as well.

00:00:49.795 --> 00:00:52.093
Also as you are writing
something down it's much

00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:54.716
more likely to stick in your head, too.

00:00:54.716 --> 00:00:56.013
Enjoy.

00:00:56.013 --> 00:00:58.824
(low digital swoosh)

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:02.991
(intense, inspiring orchestral music)

00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:07.254
(digital bleep)

00:01:08.972 --> 00:01:10.530
- There's this book called The One Thing,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.363
have you ever seen that?

00:01:11.363 --> 00:01:12.284
It says you should just do one thing.

00:01:12.284 --> 00:01:14.047
And I go, "Well I shouldn't
even bother reading that,

00:01:14.047 --> 00:01:15.734
"that doesn't apply to
me," but then (chuckles)

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.941
I thought about it and it's
like, "Well I do one thing.

00:01:17.941 --> 00:01:19.687
"I live a creative life."

00:01:19.687 --> 00:01:22.577
Everything you do you
apply creativity to it

00:01:22.577 --> 00:01:24.401
whether it's the way
you meet with a friend

00:01:24.401 --> 00:01:27.181
and make them dinner or you
do stuff with your kids.

00:01:27.181 --> 00:01:29.313
And then when you go and
you're working on an idea

00:01:29.313 --> 00:01:30.690
and you're doing music at the same time

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:31.993
while you're on the set.

00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:33.848
You're never in a block
because you're always

00:01:33.848 --> 00:01:35.161
actually in a flow.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.619
If you don't live your life creatively

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:39.551
and then you set aside some time at night

00:01:39.551 --> 00:01:41.645
to go write your novel,
you're going to get a block

00:01:41.645 --> 00:01:43.220
because you're not in the flow.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.289
But if you apply creativity
to everything in your life

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:47.952
everything is available to you

00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:49.731
to where you're suddenly can paint.

00:01:49.731 --> 00:01:51.753
Like you'll see paintings
I did with the actors.

00:01:51.753 --> 00:01:53.430
Even though they're very
creative in the acting world

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.768
they didn't think they could paint.

00:01:54.768 --> 00:01:56.134
So I made them paint and
then they saw that they

00:01:56.134 --> 00:01:58.960
were able to because when
you're in a creative flow

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.729
you can do just about anything.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:03.884
So it's all about living
a creative life to where

00:02:03.884 --> 00:02:08.135
you can't possibly ever get
blocked because you're always,

00:02:08.135 --> 00:02:09.680
"Okay well I have an idea for writing

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.449
"so I'm going to go cook something
which is art you can eat."

00:02:12.449 --> 00:02:13.613
You know and you're serving
it and you're eating it

00:02:13.613 --> 00:02:14.992
and consuming and you're creating

00:02:14.992 --> 00:02:16.348
and you go back and you do that.

00:02:16.348 --> 00:02:17.226
Does that make sense it's almost like?

00:02:17.226 --> 00:02:18.059
- Yeah there's no,

00:02:18.059 --> 00:02:19.268
there's no structured boxes around it.

00:02:19.268 --> 00:02:20.781
- There's no
- It's like a lifestyle.

00:02:20.781 --> 00:02:22.657
- Billion nation where
we're doing work and play

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:23.850
it's all play.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.772
Thinking completely different is the key.

00:02:25.772 --> 00:02:27.535
If everyone is competing on one level

00:02:27.535 --> 00:02:29.398
you got to think of another.

00:02:29.398 --> 00:02:32.401
Like for instance I'd always
wanted to get in television.

00:02:32.401 --> 00:02:33.484
But TV is so,

00:02:34.764 --> 00:02:35.751
it was always so crowded.

00:02:35.751 --> 00:02:38.244
Everyone's trying to get
in on Friday night on NBC.

00:02:38.244 --> 00:02:40.336
So what do you do when everyone?

00:02:40.336 --> 00:02:41.577
I've always heard this advice and

00:02:41.577 --> 00:02:43.065
it made sense only recently.

00:02:43.065 --> 00:02:44.266
When everyone is doing the same thing

00:02:44.266 --> 00:02:45.982
and everyone's competing in the same realm

00:02:45.982 --> 00:02:47.660
you have to think much bigger.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.680
There's a lot less competition up there.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.903
There's nobody up there, actually.

00:02:52.903 --> 00:02:53.951
- Find the hole to fill it.
- Yeah.

00:02:53.951 --> 00:02:54.944
- Instead of trying to
- So like

00:02:54.944 --> 00:02:55.777
- get into other people's territory.

00:02:55.777 --> 00:02:57.132
- I can't get my show on TV.

00:02:57.132 --> 00:02:59.268
I know I'll do a network.

00:02:59.268 --> 00:03:00.685
Then I can put on any show
(interviewer laughs)

00:03:00.685 --> 00:03:01.518
that I want.

00:03:01.518 --> 00:03:03.451
And how many other people
are going up for a network?

00:03:03.451 --> 00:03:05.240
You know how many other people
applied for that network?

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.073
- How many?

00:03:06.073 --> 00:03:07.981
- It was a lot and then it wasn't a lot.

00:03:07.981 --> 00:03:11.043
100 people in the whole country.

00:03:11.043 --> 00:03:13.379
In the whole country 100 people threw in

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:16.172
an idea for a network and
showed a presentation.

00:03:16.172 --> 00:03:17.022
That's not a lot of people.

00:03:17.022 --> 00:03:17.935
- [Interviewer] That
isn't a lot of people for

00:03:17.935 --> 00:03:19.553
how many people have ideas.
- It's a lot less people

00:03:19.553 --> 00:03:21.490
that's even in this room.
(interviewer laughs)

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.090
So when you think about that think about,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:24.998
"How can I show somebody?

00:03:24.998 --> 00:03:26.735
"How can I get someone's attention?"

00:03:26.735 --> 00:03:29.358
Everyone else is at the festival
don't go to the festival.

00:03:29.358 --> 00:03:31.379
Do think of something,
maybe go to the festival

00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:33.705
but also something else beyond that.

00:03:33.705 --> 00:03:35.490
Don't rely on the festival
'cause then you're just

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.253
crossing your fingers, you
got to hit it from other ways.

00:03:38.253 --> 00:03:39.782
And that promotes much bigger thinking.

00:03:39.782 --> 00:03:41.651
I always say aim high.

00:03:41.651 --> 00:03:43.027
'Cause if you aim just at the festival

00:03:43.027 --> 00:03:45.604
you might hit it and you
might just hit lower.

00:03:45.604 --> 00:03:47.951
But if you aim really
high you might still just

00:03:47.951 --> 00:03:50.848
hit the festival but at
least now you got a chance

00:03:50.848 --> 00:03:52.184
that you'll hit there, there or like

00:03:52.184 --> 00:03:54.161
(makes whoosh noise) straight up.

00:03:54.161 --> 00:03:56.619
So try to think as big as you can.

00:03:56.619 --> 00:03:57.786
You can't just

00:03:58.814 --> 00:04:01.167
wait for inspiration to act.

00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:03.552
That's the biggest mistake people make.

00:04:03.552 --> 00:04:05.763
Don't wait to be inspired
to do the action.

00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:06.927
You have to do the action first

00:04:06.927 --> 00:04:08.427
and then you'll be inspired.

00:04:08.427 --> 00:04:09.504
So what I would do is I would sit there

00:04:09.504 --> 00:04:10.904
and draw at the comic and
I would come up with it.

00:04:10.904 --> 00:04:11.935
But some days I'd come home from school,

00:04:11.935 --> 00:04:13.397
sit down and go, "I'm
just going to wait for

00:04:13.397 --> 00:04:14.432
"the inspiration to hit me.

00:04:14.432 --> 00:04:16.577
"I'm going to wait for the
idea to come fully formed

00:04:16.577 --> 00:04:19.435
"and when I got a joke
then I'll go draw it."

00:04:19.435 --> 00:04:21.178
And I would sit there for
maybe three or four hours

00:04:21.178 --> 00:04:22.580
(laughs) and then go, "All right screw it.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.244
"I got to get up, I got to do the process.

00:04:24.244 --> 00:04:25.997
"I got to sit there
and just start drawing.

00:04:25.997 --> 00:04:29.352
"I don't even know why or
how I'm going to get there."

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:30.907
And then two things would
come together and it would be,

00:04:30.907 --> 00:04:32.480
"Oh, there's an idea, oh
there's the first panel

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.974
"there's the second panel," and
then it would come together.

00:04:33.974 --> 00:04:36.040
Always, it would not take very long.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.682
I would fight that process so much

00:04:37.682 --> 00:04:38.515
because what was I doing?

00:04:38.515 --> 00:04:40.847
I was waiting for
inspiration before I acted.

00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:42.591
It's always the other way around.

00:04:42.591 --> 00:04:45.200
Act first, then the inspiration will come.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.672
Draw first, then the
inspiration will come.

00:04:48.672 --> 00:04:51.427
Just start shooting, then
the inspiration will come.

00:04:51.427 --> 00:04:53.655
Just start moving then
the inspiration will come.

00:04:53.655 --> 00:04:55.856
Don't wait, so many people wait

00:04:55.856 --> 00:04:57.409
or tell you they're
going to write this novel

00:04:57.409 --> 00:04:58.334
or they're going to do this.

00:04:58.334 --> 00:05:00.084
Or they're going to make
this film, they never do it.

00:05:00.084 --> 00:05:02.734
Because they're waiting for
all this stuff to come to them.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.410
It comes through doing it.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.368
Somebody was telling me,
it was actually an actor

00:05:07.368 --> 00:05:08.595
was about to do a short film and he'd been

00:05:08.595 --> 00:05:10.747
sending me the script saying,
"I'm still working on it."

00:05:10.747 --> 00:05:13.255
It was a really great idea it's kind of

00:05:13.255 --> 00:05:15.702
a big little production
for him to do, big little.

00:05:15.702 --> 00:05:17.417
And then just recently I
said, "Are you doing it?"

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:18.549
And he goes, "Ah I'm still thinking

00:05:18.549 --> 00:05:20.168
"it might be too ambitious.

00:05:20.168 --> 00:05:21.654
"I think I'm going to do
maybe something easier."

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.036
I go, "No, do the more ambitious one.

00:05:23.036 --> 00:05:25.301
"You're going to hate yourself
if you go do the easy one

00:05:25.301 --> 00:05:26.249
"and then you're dicking around with that.

00:05:26.249 --> 00:05:27.298
"You're going to be wishing you had done

00:05:27.298 --> 00:05:28.314
the more ambitious one."

00:05:28.314 --> 00:05:30.164
Do the more ambitious thing always first.

00:05:30.164 --> 00:05:32.146
And you should always
have some self doubt,

00:05:32.146 --> 00:05:34.856
otherwise it's a walk in the
park, it ain't worth doing.

00:05:34.856 --> 00:05:36.388
If it's that easy that
you're not going to be

00:05:36.388 --> 00:05:37.991
questioning yourself constantly,

00:05:37.991 --> 00:05:39.947
"Am I doing the right thing?

00:05:39.947 --> 00:05:43.707
"Is this too big a task for me to handle?

00:05:43.707 --> 00:05:45.761
"Am I not strong enough?"

00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:47.882
then you know you're
in the right direction.

00:05:47.882 --> 00:05:51.617
If you say, "I'm a fraud,
they'll figure me out.

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:52.899
"I'll never be able to do it again."

00:05:52.899 --> 00:05:54.234
That's an artist's thought process

00:05:54.234 --> 00:05:55.360
'cause you're an artist.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.037
If you're thinking like that
you're on the right track,

00:05:57.037 --> 00:05:58.139
'cause they all feel that.

00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:00.419
So there's always some
success that can come out

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:01.587
even out of your failures.

00:06:01.587 --> 00:06:03.217
In fact probably your biggest successes

00:06:03.217 --> 00:06:05.064
will come directly from your failures.

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:07.226
You should almost take
that Winston Churchill's

00:06:07.226 --> 00:06:09.710
quote, it's my favorite,
"Success is moving

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.577
"from one failure to the
next with great enthusiasm."

00:06:12.577 --> 00:06:14.860
You should be very enthusiastic
about your failures

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.536
'cause you're going to learn something.

00:06:16.536 --> 00:06:18.456
You're going to go that
way when everyone else

00:06:18.456 --> 00:06:19.505
is going that way.

00:06:19.505 --> 00:06:21.081
And you're going to stumble,
but you're also going to

00:06:21.081 --> 00:06:23.640
stumble upon something that
no one ever thought of.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.282
So you know you just keep
going and keep going.

00:06:25.282 --> 00:06:26.841
And you're going to fail and then rise up

00:06:26.841 --> 00:06:29.919
and fail again and that's the
journey, up and down, man.

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:31.100
I looked at it afterwards and I thought,

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.197
"Okay anthology films never work.

00:06:34.197 --> 00:06:36.157
"But I love the short film format."

00:06:36.157 --> 00:06:37.714
That's what I grew up
that's what Bedhead was,

00:06:37.714 --> 00:06:39.051
that's what I've done all through college.

00:06:39.051 --> 00:06:41.380
I believe in that format.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:42.852
I think the way they're doing anthologies

00:06:42.852 --> 00:06:46.087
isn't correct, four different directors

00:06:46.087 --> 00:06:47.587
that's no thematic

00:06:48.619 --> 00:06:51.755
real tie to it, four
stories it's too many.

00:06:51.755 --> 00:06:55.222
The three act structure,
classical three act structure,

00:06:55.222 --> 00:06:57.710
three, the group of three,
rule of three in comedy.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.782
It always comes in three.

00:06:59.782 --> 00:07:02.335
I bet if I did an anthology
with three stories

00:07:02.335 --> 00:07:04.776
and a wrap around with the same director,

00:07:04.776 --> 00:07:07.111
not the multiple directors, it could work.

00:07:07.111 --> 00:07:09.445
That was Sin City and I
tried it again on Sin City.

00:07:09.445 --> 00:07:11.414
Second key from that one.

00:07:11.414 --> 00:07:12.703
So looking back now it's easier for me

00:07:12.703 --> 00:07:13.736
to look and go, "Okay."

00:07:13.736 --> 00:07:15.457
But back then I was lost in the woods.

00:07:15.457 --> 00:07:17.045
I'd thought I'd made a wrong choice.

00:07:17.045 --> 00:07:19.409
I thought I'd, I followed my voice

00:07:19.409 --> 00:07:21.308
and I go, "I need to question my voice."

00:07:21.308 --> 00:07:23.152
Don't question your inner voice.

00:07:23.152 --> 00:07:25.212
That's the part that's wiser than you.

00:07:25.212 --> 00:07:27.287
That's the part that you
are not going to be able

00:07:27.287 --> 00:07:29.887
to consciously ever figure out

00:07:29.887 --> 00:07:32.922
until you look back on
it like a history book

00:07:32.922 --> 00:07:34.409
and read the map.

00:07:34.409 --> 00:07:37.458
So you have to trust your
inner voice and cultivate that.

00:07:37.458 --> 00:07:39.287
Down to the point where
you could be playing cards

00:07:39.287 --> 00:07:42.199
and going, "I know to throw
down the queen because my

00:07:42.199 --> 00:07:43.795
"inner voice told (chuckles) me to."

00:07:43.795 --> 00:07:47.164
You've got to really fine tune that gift.

00:07:47.164 --> 00:07:48.211
And it's within all of you.

00:07:48.211 --> 00:07:49.044
(digital swoosh)

00:07:49.044 --> 00:07:50.747
- So you make this little
film with some money

00:07:50.747 --> 00:07:52.300
that you got from selling your ass

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.015
in the medical community.
(audience laughs)

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:57.358
At what point does, you
put together a VHS cut

00:07:57.358 --> 00:07:58.255
if I remember correctly, right?

00:07:58.255 --> 00:08:01.683
- Yeah, I cut it, I cut it
at the public access station

00:08:01.683 --> 00:08:04.148
on 3/4 inch and then I made VHSes.

00:08:04.148 --> 00:08:06.632
And you're idea was like I'm
going to sell this in the

00:08:06.632 --> 00:08:07.465
- I was going straight
- Spanish language market

00:08:07.465 --> 00:08:08.957
- to Spanish video market.
- or something.

00:08:08.957 --> 00:08:10.040
- That's the main thing I tell people.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.506
Is that my goal was to
just go double my money.

00:08:12.506 --> 00:08:14.845
I didn't know it was going
to, you know, over perform.

00:08:14.845 --> 00:08:16.042
- [Kevin] And this cats were
like, "We could turn this

00:08:16.042 --> 00:08:17.166
"into a real feature."

00:08:17.166 --> 00:08:19.010
- Well what they wanted
to do was we were going to

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.596
remake it as Desperado.

00:08:20.596 --> 00:08:21.822
And they said, "Well before we make it

00:08:21.822 --> 00:08:24.080
"let's test the movie that
you made already on $7,000.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.708
"Let's test it to an
audience and see 'cause there

00:08:25.708 --> 00:08:26.640
"might be a down already."

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.800
And they tested it and it
tested great as it was.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.934
And they said, "Let's
take this to festivals."

00:08:30.934 --> 00:08:33.157
And I was like, "No I
made for, I didn't mean

00:08:33.157 --> 00:08:34.082
"for anybody to watch this!
(audience laughs)

00:08:34.082 --> 00:08:36.666
"Give me $2,000 I'll re-shoot half of it."

00:08:36.666 --> 00:08:37.499
(Kevin laughs)
I didn't want them

00:08:37.499 --> 00:08:38.773
to show that one 'cause that was just one

00:08:38.773 --> 00:08:40.044
I was just trying to get it out.

00:08:40.044 --> 00:08:41.604
And I learned a big lesson from that.

00:08:41.604 --> 00:08:43.950
Because I was only making
it as a throwaway film.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:45.780
It came out better than
the films I did on purpose.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:46.663
- No!

00:08:46.663 --> 00:08:48.362
- So if you throw things away like that

00:08:48.362 --> 00:08:51.342
and don't make it precious
you'll come up with stuff

00:08:51.342 --> 00:08:53.073
that you probably wouldn't have thought of

00:08:53.073 --> 00:08:55.394
if you were on, you know you were on.

00:08:55.394 --> 00:08:56.999
- El Mariachi was that shot on 16?

00:08:56.999 --> 00:08:58.098
- 16 millimeter.

00:08:58.098 --> 00:08:59.373
- So its like now it's like the camera

00:08:59.373 --> 00:09:01.115
that you carry around in
your pocket is a better

00:09:01.115 --> 00:09:03.137
camera than you were
probably scraping to get

00:09:03.137 --> 00:09:04.269
back in those days.

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:06.286
- Yeah man, I tell people if
I shot the same move today

00:09:06.286 --> 00:09:08.904
it would probably be like
a $700 movie, not a $7,000.

00:09:08.904 --> 00:09:10.259
Most of that budget went to,

00:09:10.259 --> 00:09:12.056
the majority of the budget went to film.

00:09:12.056 --> 00:09:14.280
Buying the film, processing
it, transferring it

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.784
to video, so yeah you
could shoot the same movie

00:09:16.784 --> 00:09:18.167
for nothing today.
- How many

00:09:18.167 --> 00:09:19.187
crew were on that.

00:09:19.187 --> 00:09:20.020
- I was the whole crew.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.022
- Oh yeah I was the sound guy,
- You did one man crew?

00:09:22.022 --> 00:09:23.907
- I was the camera guy,
I was the lighting guy.

00:09:23.907 --> 00:09:24.740
- Director.
- 'Cause I didn't know

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:26.919
if I could sell it, so I
didn't want to spend more.

00:09:26.919 --> 00:09:29.368
It was already a lot and
costing years to do it.

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:31.552
And I knew kind of what I
could do just by myself.

00:09:31.552 --> 00:09:34.409
- So is it hard going
from one man crew to then

00:09:34.409 --> 00:09:36.602
giving all those
responsibilities to other people?

00:09:36.602 --> 00:09:37.631
Or is it just a load off your mind?

00:09:37.631 --> 00:09:39.273
- Well that's why I still
do a lot of jobs myself.

00:09:39.273 --> 00:09:41.151
I actually ended up enjoying those.

00:09:41.151 --> 00:09:43.890
So I still write, produce, direct, edit,

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.420
operate the camera, I do the sound mix,

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.383
I do the score.

00:09:46.383 --> 00:09:47.899
I mean I still do a lot of those jobs

00:09:47.899 --> 00:09:50.818
'cause I discovered that on the first one

00:09:50.818 --> 00:09:51.909
that it was just so fun.

00:09:51.909 --> 00:09:53.883
And now I think a lot of,
it's a lot more relatable

00:09:53.883 --> 00:09:55.162
to people today.

00:09:55.162 --> 00:09:56.430
I was on that new age
where I was coming in

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:58.700
and I learned on video, learned on digital

00:09:58.700 --> 00:09:59.866
and then I shot film.

00:09:59.866 --> 00:10:01.596
And I applied what I
learned on digital to film

00:10:01.596 --> 00:10:03.125
which was just do it yourself.

00:10:03.125 --> 00:10:04.986
You know so many people
today now shoot that way

00:10:04.986 --> 00:10:06.475
because they shoot digital.

00:10:06.475 --> 00:10:08.763
But that was unheard of in the
film world to do it that way.

00:10:08.763 --> 00:10:10.270
You always needed like 200 people.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:11.103
(digital swoosh)

00:10:11.103 --> 00:10:12.138
When you wake up every morning

00:10:12.138 --> 00:10:14.166
you need to know why?

00:10:14.166 --> 00:10:15.416
Start with why.

00:10:16.585 --> 00:10:18.404
What are you right now, you're students?

00:10:18.404 --> 00:10:19.987
That's what you do.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.183
How do you do that?

00:10:22.183 --> 00:10:23.574
You go to school, does
anybody taking online courses

00:10:23.574 --> 00:10:25.209
or is it all classes like this?

00:10:25.209 --> 00:10:27.314
Classes like this, that's how you do it.

00:10:27.314 --> 00:10:29.471
So many people start from what they do

00:10:29.471 --> 00:10:30.981
to how they do it.

00:10:30.981 --> 00:10:33.562
Very few people think about why.

00:10:33.562 --> 00:10:35.729
You got to start with why.

00:10:36.836 --> 00:10:39.317
So let me explain why that's important.

00:10:39.317 --> 00:10:40.722
Because if many of you know
why you're doing something

00:10:40.722 --> 00:10:43.224
you can attain things much
better and much clearer.

00:10:43.224 --> 00:10:47.088
Your decision making
becomes much more important.

00:10:47.088 --> 00:10:48.505
Much easier, too.

00:10:49.512 --> 00:10:53.780
Apple for instance does it like perfectly.

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:56.571
When you have a computer company
that just makes computers

00:10:56.571 --> 00:10:57.404
they say, "What do you do?"

00:10:57.404 --> 00:10:58.434
"I make computers."

00:10:58.434 --> 00:10:59.492
"How do you do it?"

00:10:59.492 --> 00:11:01.872
"I use the highest grade of technology,

00:11:01.872 --> 00:11:03.446
"the coolest product,
the coolest guy who's

00:11:03.446 --> 00:11:04.827
"going to speak to you.

00:11:04.827 --> 00:11:06.071
"That's how we do it.

00:11:06.071 --> 00:11:07.339
"Do you want to buy a computer from me?"

00:11:07.339 --> 00:11:08.960
"No."

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.179
Apple starts the other way they start with

00:11:10.179 --> 00:11:11.269
why they're doing it.

00:11:11.269 --> 00:11:12.828
"We think different.

00:11:12.828 --> 00:11:14.349
"We think you think differently too.

00:11:14.349 --> 00:11:15.745
"We think you're an individual.

00:11:15.745 --> 00:11:17.269
"And you see one of our
ads, there's not 10 people

00:11:17.269 --> 00:11:19.316
"enjoying our computer there's one.

00:11:19.316 --> 00:11:20.789
"We want to make product that speaks

00:11:20.789 --> 00:11:22.255
"to your individuality."

00:11:22.255 --> 00:11:23.748
"How you going to do that?"

00:11:23.748 --> 00:11:25.103
Well already you believe that like,

00:11:25.103 --> 00:11:26.818
"Yeah I'm an individual
I think differently

00:11:26.818 --> 00:11:27.709
"than other people.

00:11:27.709 --> 00:11:29.617
"I want something that's
customized to me."

00:11:29.617 --> 00:11:31.242
"How you going to do that?"

00:11:31.242 --> 00:11:33.786
"We use the best technology
that makes the sexiest

00:11:33.786 --> 00:11:35.021
"equipment that you're going to want."

00:11:35.021 --> 00:11:35.854
"What do you do?"

00:11:35.854 --> 00:11:37.669
"I sell computers or I sell
phones do you want one?"

00:11:37.669 --> 00:11:38.502
"Yes."

00:11:38.502 --> 00:11:40.232
Talk about why, why are you doing it?

00:11:40.232 --> 00:11:41.805
So when people wanted to compete with me

00:11:41.805 --> 00:11:43.100
with the network and they
didn't get the network

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:44.537
and they say, "Why did
we not get the network?"

00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:48.037
I say, "Why did you want to do a network?"

00:11:49.077 --> 00:11:51.196
It comes down to always
like a business reason.

00:11:51.196 --> 00:11:52.492
"Well we made a bunch of movies

00:11:52.492 --> 00:11:54.327
"and we don't have a TV
deal and if I had a network

00:11:54.327 --> 00:11:55.772
"then I could sell my own movies to myself

00:11:55.772 --> 00:11:56.652
"and make money that way."

00:11:56.652 --> 00:11:58.129
"Well there, that's not good enough."

00:11:58.129 --> 00:11:58.962
(audience laughs)

00:11:58.962 --> 00:12:00.662
You know you don't deserve to be there.

00:12:00.662 --> 00:12:02.854
You got to have a good
reason why you're doing it.

00:12:02.854 --> 00:12:04.765
So if you clarify why
you're doing anything

00:12:04.765 --> 00:12:07.836
you'll find that the rest of
the success comes much easier.

00:12:07.836 --> 00:12:09.710
I remember I gave this talk to

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.445
a group of entrepreneurs.

00:12:11.445 --> 00:12:13.330
And this one woman raised
her hand and she goes,

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.190
"Well you're real positive.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:17.314
"But what do you do, what
do you just tell yourself

00:12:17.314 --> 00:12:19.223
"when you've just wasted a year and a half

00:12:19.223 --> 00:12:21.809
"of your life doing something
and it didn't work out."

00:12:21.809 --> 00:12:23.882
And I told her,

00:12:23.882 --> 00:12:24.715
"Well that's a real negative way

00:12:24.715 --> 00:12:25.938
"of looking at it." (laughs)

00:12:25.938 --> 00:12:27.430
- I didn't that already.
(audience laughs)

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:29.245
- Why can you rephrase the question

00:12:29.245 --> 00:12:30.551
and then ask me again?

00:12:30.551 --> 00:12:31.851
'Cause it's just you're looking at it

00:12:31.851 --> 00:12:33.245
really negatively first of all,

00:12:33.245 --> 00:12:34.588
so I won't even answer it that way.

00:12:34.588 --> 00:12:35.822
- So you asked her--
- So she thought about it

00:12:35.822 --> 00:12:37.642
and she said, "Well I learned an important

00:12:37.642 --> 00:12:39.093
"lesson the hard way."

00:12:39.093 --> 00:12:42.105
And I said, "That still sucks.

00:12:42.105 --> 00:12:43.235
"No wonder you don't want to move forward

00:12:43.235 --> 00:12:45.335
"because you feel like
you've done something wrong

00:12:45.335 --> 00:12:46.602
"because it didn't work out the way

00:12:46.602 --> 00:12:49.510
"you had predetermined you
wanted it to work out."

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:50.948
You have to look at it a different way.

00:12:50.948 --> 00:12:52.471
If you feel like you want to do something

00:12:52.471 --> 00:12:53.644
you should just step forward.

00:12:53.644 --> 00:12:55.214
It may not work,

00:12:55.214 --> 00:12:58.026
but you're going to hit
a step in your career

00:12:58.026 --> 00:13:00.525
and in your development that
you could not have taken

00:13:00.525 --> 00:13:02.852
had you not gone that way,
before you can get over there.

00:13:02.852 --> 00:13:05.364
And I'd say in the ashes
of all your failures

00:13:05.364 --> 00:13:08.050
if you look close enough
you'll find keys to success.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:09.643
If you're a very negative person all you

00:13:09.643 --> 00:13:12.344
would focus on is, "Wow
I have a really bad movie

00:13:12.344 --> 00:13:14.928
"on my resume that I
wish I had never done."

00:13:14.928 --> 00:13:16.781
But if you're a positive person you'll go,

00:13:16.781 --> 00:13:19.592
"I'm so glad I took that
step that most people

00:13:19.592 --> 00:13:21.685
"would not have taken 'cause
that got me to a place

00:13:21.685 --> 00:13:24.185
"that no one else can follow."

00:13:26.109 --> 00:13:27.443
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:27.443 --> 00:13:30.426
I made this video because
Higher Images Official

00:13:30.426 --> 00:13:32.434
asked me to, so if there's
a famous entrepreneur

00:13:32.434 --> 00:13:34.233
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:34.233 --> 00:13:35.547
leave it down in the comments below

00:13:35.547 --> 00:13:36.709
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:36.709 --> 00:13:38.346
I'd also love to know what did Robert say

00:13:38.346 --> 00:13:39.957
that had the biggest impact on you?

00:13:39.957 --> 00:13:41.654
What are you going to take from this video

00:13:41.654 --> 00:13:44.156
and immediately apply
it somehow to your life

00:13:44.156 --> 00:13:45.359
or to your business.

00:13:45.359 --> 00:13:46.467
Leave it down in the comments.

00:13:46.467 --> 00:13:48.013
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:13:48.013 --> 00:13:49.308
Finally I want to give a quick shout out

00:13:49.308 --> 00:13:51.330
to Daniel and Alex, thank you guys so much

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:52.786
for picking up a copy of my book,

00:13:52.786 --> 00:13:55.188
Your One Word, and making
that awesome tribute

00:13:55.188 --> 00:13:56.858
website for it as well.

00:13:56.858 --> 00:13:58.516
I really, really, really appreciate it.

00:13:58.516 --> 00:14:00.725
So thank you guys again for
watching I believe in you.

00:14:00.725 --> 00:14:02.788
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:02.788 --> 00:14:06.044
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:14:06.044 --> 00:14:07.597
I'll see you soon.

00:14:07.597 --> 00:14:11.014
(intense digital swoosh)

00:14:13.488 --> 00:14:15.400
- Usually you're always
hurting for money in a budget

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:16.657
to put any ideas together.

00:14:16.657 --> 00:14:18.145
So if you're making this
cool sketch you're like,

00:14:18.145 --> 00:14:21.305
"Okay well, we'll never afford
to be able to make this."

00:14:21.305 --> 00:14:22.808
Well that's the opposite with this.

00:14:22.808 --> 00:14:25.755
We'll have like a whole
team ready to make that

00:14:25.755 --> 00:14:27.214
vision come to life.

00:14:27.214 --> 00:14:28.967
And the whole idea is
that it's your vision.

00:14:28.967 --> 00:14:31.009
Because I've done my
part and now I'm waiting

00:14:31.009 --> 00:14:32.764
for those missing pieces 'cause

00:14:32.764 --> 00:14:34.384
I love collaborating with
people because you're

00:14:34.384 --> 00:14:36.674
always wondering what part
they're going to bring.

00:14:36.674 --> 00:14:39.195
Whether it's an actor or
someone you interact with.

00:14:39.195 --> 00:14:40.960
They bring a piece of
magic that you couldn't

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:42.973
have generated yourself and you take it

00:14:42.973 --> 00:14:46.079
and you run with it and you're so excited

00:14:46.079 --> 00:14:48.455
to incorporate their ideas.

00:14:48.455 --> 00:14:49.701
- Now you had a plan though.

00:14:49.701 --> 00:14:53.249
The remarkable thing to
me was at 22, 23 years old

00:14:53.249 --> 00:14:55.157
that you're plan was completely laid out.

00:14:55.157 --> 00:14:56.851
I mean in reading your book.

00:14:56.851 --> 00:14:58.347
You knew I was going to
take this amount of money,

00:14:58.347 --> 00:15:00.388
your plan was to make
three El Mariachi movies.

00:15:00.388 --> 00:15:02.187
- Well the thing is if
you want to get somewhere

00:15:02.187 --> 00:15:03.835
you really got to set
some goals for yourself.

00:15:03.835 --> 00:15:04.776
- Right.
- It won't ever happen

00:15:04.776 --> 00:15:05.770
the way you plan. (chuckles)

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:07.317
But at least you'll (chuckles)
- Don't I know that.

00:15:07.317 --> 00:15:10.008
(audience laughs)
set forth on positive,

00:15:10.008 --> 00:15:11.269
in a positive direction that may take you

00:15:11.269 --> 00:15:12.734
someplace better by accident.

00:15:12.734 --> 00:15:14.773
- You're working on things now that are

00:15:14.773 --> 00:15:17.045
I mean huge, it's like you're
running an entire network.

00:15:17.045 --> 00:15:18.335
You're running the El Rey network now.

00:15:18.335 --> 00:15:20.628
- Yeah, but it's a matter
of skill not technique.

00:15:20.628 --> 00:15:22.599
It's the same technique
but you're just doing it.

00:15:22.599 --> 00:15:24.648
You know you're only
really just scaling up.

00:15:24.648 --> 00:15:27.255
You're really, like
you have rooster teeth.

00:15:27.255 --> 00:15:29.097
It's like you create a beast and then

00:15:29.097 --> 00:15:32.104
you got to feed the beast
or the beast consumes you.

00:15:32.104 --> 00:15:34.940
(interviewer laughs)
And by creating for that beast

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:36.496
you actually come up with
things that you never

00:15:36.496 --> 00:15:38.996
would have done otherwise
and you're really proud of.

00:15:38.996 --> 00:15:41.327
Like our network we
have we had to fill it.

00:15:41.327 --> 00:15:42.681
I got a network, I own a network,

00:15:42.681 --> 00:15:44.190
but it's empty until you fill it.

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:46.977
So we had to fill it
with From Dusk Til Dawn,

00:15:46.977 --> 00:15:48.830
had to come up with a show
called The Director's Chair.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:50.649
Had to go with Lucha Underground

00:15:50.649 --> 00:15:51.749
and you just start filling it up

00:15:51.749 --> 00:15:53.510
and then you're so proud of these shows

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:54.603
and so proud of the content.

00:15:54.603 --> 00:15:56.413
Just like as proud as you all or anything

00:15:56.413 --> 00:15:57.264
that you just come up with.

00:15:57.264 --> 00:15:59.935
It was because you had this destination

00:15:59.935 --> 00:16:01.316
that you had to fill.

00:16:01.316 --> 00:16:04.638
So I would suggest to anybody
who wants to go create,

00:16:04.638 --> 00:16:06.878
create a beast first, then feed the beast.

00:16:06.878 --> 00:16:07.711
- There you go. (laughs)
(Robert laughs)

